er sort; per the rule, times are consecutive integers).\n\nActually, for safety, I should pair on actual time values: for each cell build a time→index mapping and only pair if both t and t+lag exist. But if data is on a regular grid, positional approach works. I'll go with a robust approach: group by cell, sort by time, verify time is consecutive integers starting from the minimum, then do positional pairing. Let me include a verification step.\n\nOK, let me write the script.\n\nAbout the report: I need actual numbers. Run the analysis first, then write the report with real values.\n\nLet me write analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Enhancer–promoter 3D distance and transcription dynamics analysis...\"\"\"\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nCONTACT_THRESHOLD_NM = 260.0\nLAG_MIN, LAG_MAX = -20, 20\nINPUT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / \"inputs\" / \"single_cell_dynamics_question.csv\"\nOUTPUT_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / \"output\"\n```\n\nWait — analysis.py will be placed at output/analysis.py. Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] would be the workspace root. But if someone runs it from the workspace root as `python output/analysis.py`, the __file__-based paths work. But to be safe, should I use paths relative to the current working directory? Hmm. The rule says deliverables go under output/. Let me make it work when run from workspace root (python output/analysis.py) with __file__-based robustness. Use a fallback: try __file__-based path first; if not found, try ./inputs.\n\nActually simpler: define ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent (workspace root) and INPUT = ROOT/\"inputs\"/..., OUTPUT_DIR = ROOT/\"output\". This is deterministic regardless of cwd. Good.\n\nThe script will:\n\n1. Load the CSV.\n2. Validate: expected columns.\n3. Compute distance and contact.\n4. Per-cell metrics → cell_metrics.csv.\n5. Lag analysis → lag_analysis.csv.\n6. Print summary statistics for the